# Group 4 — Design of Gate `N2` (Mechanized Vacuity on Spec & Proof Branches)

## 1. Architectural Constraint: `ConcreteSpec` Purity (`trust_ban.sh`) vs Annotations

Before choosing an annotation mechanism, inspect [ConcreteSpec/README.md](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/prop-agy/ConcreteSpec/README.md#L31) and [scripts/proofs/trust_ban.sh](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/prop-agy/scripts/proofs/trust_ban.sh#L38-L44) ([scripts/proofs/lean_lex.py](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/prop-agy/scripts/proofs/lean_lex.py#L126-L127)):
- `ConcreteSpec/**/*.lean` may **not** `import Lean` (outside imports are banned so `ConcreteSpec` depends only on Lean's `Init` prelude), and `lean_lex.py` bans `syntax`, `macro`, `elab`, `attribute`, `#eval`, and `run_cmd` inside `ConcreteSpec`.
- Meanwhile, [scripts/proofs/theorems.lean](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/prop-agy/scripts/proofs/theorems.lean#L45-L46) lives outside `ConcreteSpec/`, imports both `Lean` and `ConcreteSpec`, and inspects the compiled `Lean.Environment` directly via `Lean.importModules`.

To honour **both** "one home, in the spec source, no side list" **and** "zero `import Lean` / metaprogramming inside `ConcreteSpec`", we have two clean options (and we can support both seamlessly):

### Recommended Annotation Scheme (Pure `Init` Attribute Tags via a Tiny `ConcreteSpec/Attr.lean` OR Structured Tag Declarations)

1. **Option 1 (Attribute syntax `@[law]`, `@[witness law]`, `@[refutes law]` via a 20-line `ConcreteSpec/Attr.lean`):**
   - Place attribute registrations in a single tiny file `ConcreteSpec/Attr.lean` (the **only** file allowed by `trust_ban.sh:44` and `imports.sh` to `import Lean.Attributes`, excluded from `Spec/*` and `Proofs/*` semantic files just as `theorems.lean` is):
     ```lean
     import Lean.Attributes
     namespace ConcreteSpec
     initialize lawAttr : Lean.TagAttribute ←
       Lean.registerTagAttribute `law "Contract law subject to N2 vacuity checking"
     initialize lawExemptAttr : Lean.ParametricAttribute String ←
       Lean.registerParametricAttribute {
         name := `law_exempt
         descr := "Reviewer-approved N2 exemption with mandatory reason string"
         getParam := fun _ stx => match stx with
           | `(attr| law_exempt $s:str) => pure s.getString
           | _ => throwError "usage: @[law_exempt \"<reason and reviewer>\"]" }
     initialize witnessAttr : Lean.ParametricAttribute Lean.Name ←
       Lean.registerParametricAttribute {
         name := `witness
         descr := "Non-trivial satisfiability witness for a @[law] theorem"
         getParam := fun _ stx => match stx with
           | `(attr| witness $id:ident) => Lean.realizeGlobalConstNoOverload id
           | _ => throwError "usage: @[witness <lawName>]" }
     initialize refutesAttr : Lean.ParametricAttribute (Lean.Name × Option Lean.Name) ←
       Lean.registerParametricAttribute {
         name := `refutes
         descr := "Behaviour-refuting counter-example for a @[law] theorem"
         getParam := fun _ stx => match stx with
           | `(attr| refutes $law:ident) => do
               pure (← Lean.realizeGlobalConstNoOverload law, none)
           | `(attr| refutes $law:ident via $mutDef:ident) => do
               pure (← Lean.realizeGlobalConstNoOverload law,
                     some (← Lean.realizeGlobalConstNoOverload mutDef))
           | _ => throwError "usage: @[refutes <lawName>] or @[refutes <lawName> via <mutatedDef>]" }
     end ConcreteSpec
     ```
   - Notice `Lean.realizeGlobalConstNoOverload` resolves `@[witnesscheck_complete]` or `@[refutes borrow_exclusive]` to its fully-qualified `Lean.Name` at elaboration time—so a typo in the law name is an immediate compile error!
   - In `scripts/proofs/lean_lex.py:126` and `trust_ban.sh:44`, `ConcreteSpec/Attr.lean` is whitelisted exclusively for those 4 `register*Attribute` calls, while `ConcreteSpec/Spec/**` and `ConcreteSpec/Proofs/**` remain 100% barred from `import Lean`, `macro`, `elab`, `syntax`, `local instance`, etc.

2. **Completeness Rule ("No Silent Unannotated Laws"):**
   What stops a writer from simply omitting `@[law]` on a new theorem in `ConcreteSpec/Proofs/*` or `ConcreteSpec/Spec/*`?
   - Gate `N2` scans **every** `ConstantInfo.thmInfo` in `ConcreteSpec.Spec.*` and `ConcreteSpec.Proofs.*`.
   - Every theorem must carry **exactly one** role attribute:
     - `@[law]` — a contract law or headline theorem (must pass `v1`, `v2`, and `v3`);
     - `@[witness <law>]` — a witness theorem/example for `<law>`;
     - `@[refutes <law>]` — a behaviour-refuting counterexample for `<law>`;
     - `@[helper]` (or `private theorem`) — an internal plumbing lemma (still checked for `v1` so it is not a projection tautology, and required to appear in the proof DAG of at least one `@[law]` theorem so dead/orphan lemmas cannot masquerade as helpers!);
     - `@[law_exempt "<kind>: <reason> (approved: <reviewer>, <date>)"]` — an explicit, reviewer-approved exemption.
   - Any `theorem` in `ConcreteSpec` lacking one of these attributes **fails Gate `N2` immediately** with `UNCLASSIFIED-THEOREM: <name>`. There is **no side list** anywhere outside `.lean` source.

